n26|\n27|## G-A (blocking) — the arm enumeration is still manual, and it is paying rent\n28|\n29|**Answer: still manual.** The rev1→rev2 delta is test-only —\n30|`internal/provhost/inventory_test.go` is byte-identical between the two\n31|candidate trees, and no production file changed. Named findings got named\n32|tests; nothing enumerates refusal branches from production.\n33|\n34|Measured directly by planting a new refusal branch and asking whether anything\n35|reddens:\n36|\n37|| Planted branch | Shape | Suite |\n38|| --- | --- | ---: |\n39|| G2 — new `failProtocol` **call site** in `DecodeResponse` | new constructor call site | **KILLED** — audit: \"refusal call sites without an exercised negative path: protocol.go:388\" |\n40|| G4 — new `failInvalid` **call site** in `encodeFrame` | new constructor call site | **KILLED** by the same audit |\n41|| G1 — new `&frameFault{detail: \"member is too long\", member: \"protocol_version\"}` in `checkResponseMembers` | new **arm** through an existing site | **SURVIVED** |\n42|| G3 — new `integrity(\"status state is too long\", state)` in `DecodeStatusOutcome` | new **arm** through the single `failIntegrity` site | **SURVIVED** |\n43|\n44|So the mechanism enumerates *constructor call sites*, exactly as round 1\n45|described, and is still blind to arm-slide by construction: all 10 `frameFault`\n46|literals funnel through two `failProtocol` sites, and all 18 `integrity(...)`\n47|arms funnel through one `failIntegrity` site at `status.go:77`.\n48|\n49|Arm coverage, as a measured ratio rather than prose: **51 of 52** production\n50|refusal arms are named by at least one assertion. One is not (X19, below); the\n51|`failMismatch` arm is identified by its `observed` detail rather than its human\n52|detail, which is fine.\n53|\n54|**But arm identity was never the whole gap.** `parseMajor` has five *rejection\n55|branches*, and they are not refusal arms at all — they are classification\n56|branches that route between two arms. No amount of member/detail assertion\n57|reaches them; each needs its own fixture, enumerated by hand. Round 1 named\n58|four. The producer closed exactly those four. The fifth is still open:\n59|\n60|### F30 (blocking) — `parseMajor`'s non-digit **major** branch is deletable, and flips exit 13 → exit 6\n61|\n62|`internal/provhost/protocol.go:346-352`. Narrowing `if digit < '0' || digit > '9'`\n63|to a never-true condition leaves the whole suite green. Driven through\n64|`DecodeResponse`:\n65|\n66|| `protocol_version` | baseline | mutant |\n67|| --- | --- | --- |\n68|| `\"a.0.0\"` | `provider_protocol_error` exit **13** | `incompatible_protocol` exit **6** |\n69|| `\"2a.0.0\"` | `provider_protocol_error` exit 13 | `incompatible_protocol` exit 6 |\n70|| `\"-1.0.0\"` | `provider_protocol_error` exit 13 | `incompatible_protocol` exit 6 |\n71|| `\"+3.0.0\"` | `provider_protocol_error` exit 13 | `incompatible_protocol` exit 6 |\n72|\n73|Identical class and identical consequence to round 1's F21: a malformed version\n74|promoted to a recognizable major, reported through the opposite Section 15.1\n75|branch with the opposite exit code. It hides for the same reason F21 hid —\n76|every fixture with a non-numeric first character (`v2`, `a.b.c`, `3.b.c`) also\n77|has a non-numeric *rest*, so the `M22` branch catches it first and the\n78|misclassification collapses back to the same code. `.0.0` covers the empty\n79|major, nothing covers a non-empty non-numeric one.\n80|\n81|This is the round adding an nth named row and the (n+1)th arm still paying\n82|rent, which is precisely what G-A asked about.\n83|\n84|### F31 (blocking) — `stdoutCap`'s probe byte is load-bearing and unpinned; a one-byte narrowing admits a frame Section 7.2 forbids\n85|\n86|`internal/provhost/runner.go:20`, used only at `runner.go:85`. The comment at\n87|`runner.go:15-19` states the `+2` exactly: \"one maximal frame, its line\n88|terminator, and one probe byte, so a maximal frame followed by any trailing\n89|byte is still distinguishable from a clean end.\" Nothing tests it. Both\n90|directions survive; the narrowing is not cosmetic. Measured through production\n91|`ExecRunner` + `Host.Call`, with a real plugin emitting an 8 MiB frame, a\n92|terminator, and one junk byte:\n93|\n94|| `stdoutCap` | outcome |\n95|| --- | --- |\n96|| `MaxFrameBytes + 2` (as written) | `provider_protocol_error: stdout carries more than one frame` |\n97|| `MaxFrameBytes + 1` | **accepted**, `err=nil`, body 8 388 472 bytes |\n98|| `MaxFrameBytes * 4` | accepted-as-baseline; 4x the memory bound, suite green |\n99|\n100|A gate that admits what it must reject, with the suite green: \"Stdout MUST\n101|contain protocol frames only\" (§7.2, pinned by\n102|`TestSection72FramingIsPinned`). The existing `TestCallOversizeStdoutRefuses`\n103|and the `extra`-line row both drive `scriptRunner`, whose `Result.Stdout` is\n104|handed over directly and never passes through `readCapped`, so no test reaches\n105|this constant at all.\n106|\n107|## Round 1's survivors — all 18 closed\n108|\n109|| Round-1 finding | Mutants | Round-2 result |\n110|| --- | --- | --- |\n111|| F21 `parseMajor` | M19, M20, M21, M22 | **4/4 killed** — each reddens `TestDecodeResponseRefusals/foreign_*` by *code*, naming the promotion, not by landing somewhere |\n112|| F22 token floor | `<16`, `<8`, `<48` | **3/3 killed**; I added `<31` and `<33` — both killed. Floor pinned two-sided at 31 refused / 32 accepted |\n113|| F23 `readCapped` | R09 (limiter), R07 (truncation) | **2/2 killed** by `TestReadCappedStopsConsumingAtTheCap` / `TestReadCappedEnforcesCap` |\n114|| F24 request bound | M06c halve | **killed**; I added widen-2x and `>=` (exact-boundary narrow) — both killed. Response side re-checked: both killed |\n115|| F25 arm identity | M09, M09a, S02, S02a, M27 | **5/5 killed**, and they fail by *naming the slid arm*: \"error = …: not a provider envelope, want detail containing \\\"missing member\\\"\" |\n116|| F25 | M09b (drop `\"ok\"`) | **equivalent, confirmed** — the missing-`ok` pre-check at `protocol.go:403` fires first on every input; the list entry is unreachable |\n117|| F26 exit plumbing | R17 single-site | **equivalent, confirmed** — I cleared the *other* site and it also survives; each alone is masked by the other |\n118|| F26 | R17 both sites | **killed** by `TestExecRunnerReportsCrashExit/crash_exit`, reproducing round 1's exact `process_failed` → `protocol_error` flip through production `ExecRunner` |\n119|| F27 terminator | R14 | **killed** by `TestExecRunnerWritesOneTerminatedFrame` |\n120|| F28 README paragraph | — | **fixed**; the discovery paragraph is back above `## Provider Plugin JSONL Protocol` at README:418-424, and the contradictory six/three constructor counts no longer sit adjacent |\n121|| F29 evidence claim | — | **restated** to what is pinned (arm identities, 31/32 token bytes, both exact frame bounds). Each clause of the new sentence reproduces under mutation |\n122|\n123|## Resurrections: none\n124|\n125|- 23 of 23 distinct provhost tests that killed a round-1 mutant still exist by\n126|  name in the rev2 test files; the rewrite deleted no killer. The other 9\n127|  round-1 killer tests live in `internal/provider`, which rev2 does not touch.\n128|- The three inherited closers re-probed from scratch, not accepted from round 1:\n129|  F18 — a production `init()` reassigning `ownerAttester` with the correct\n130|  `func(os.FileInfo) (uint32, error)` signature turns\n131|  `TestOwnerAttesterIsUnreassigned` red; F19 — `return nil,` → `return out,` at\n132|  **all five** `Discover` refusal returns reddens\n133|  `TestDiscoverRefusesRelativePluginDir`, `…RefusesDuplicates`,\n134|  `…EnforcesTrustGatesAcrossSources`; F20 — a Windows-only type error passes\n135|  `go build` (0), `go vet` (0) and `go test ./...` (0) and is caught only by\n136|  `GOOS=windows go vet ./...` (1), the step this CR adds to CI.\n137|- Every round-1 gate class I re-derived independently is killed. What I did not\n138|  do is replay round 1's 66 rows by their original definitions — they were not\n139|  recorded as a list. I re-derived a larger traversal instead and say so.\n140|\n141|## Remaining real survivors (11 of 106; 6 more confirmed equivalent)\n142|\n143|Beyond F30 and F31:\n144|\n145|- **X19** — `integrity(\"status operation_id is not a string\")`, `status.go:118`.\n146|  The only production arm with no assertion anywhere. Narrowing it slides to the\n147|  UUIDv7 arm, same code, different detail, suite green. `status_test.go:188`\n148|  covers the malformed operation_id but not the non-string one.\n149|- **AS6** — blanking the `status_state` detail on the token-floor arm survives.\n150|  `status_state` is asserted on exactly **one** row (the unknown row,\n151|  `status_test.go:90`); `requireLocalRefusal` does not check it, so the other\n152|  17 integrity arms carry an unasserted detail.\n153|- **S4b** — `stdoutCap` widened 4x (see F31).\n154|- **S11** `WaitDelay = 0`, **S13** the `ctx.Err()` pipe-close block at\n155|  `runner.go:101-112`, **S12** the short-write check at `runner.go:93`. All three\n156|  guard detached-descendant and partial-write paths that no test constructs.\n157|  Stated bounds, not defects.\n158|- **U2** — removing the process-group `SIGKILL` `Cancel` from\n159|  `runner_unix.go:19-21` (keeping `Setpgid`) leaves the suite green. The comment\n160|  at `runner_test.go:484-487` claims the 300 ms timing proves the whole group\n161|  died — it does not: with the group kill removed I measured **300 ms** on the\n162|  same fixture, and on a `(sleep 2; touch marker) & sleep 30` plugin the\n163|  grandchild died in **both** arms. What the test does pin is that `Setpgid` and\n164|  `Kill(-pid)` are consistent (U1, `Setpgid: false`, is killed at 2.31 s). Whether\n165|  the group kill buys anything beyond that is **unknown** on macOS from my\n166|  probes — I could not construct a distinguishing case, and I am reporting that\n167|  as unknown rather than inferring it either way. The comment's specific claim\n168|  should be corrected to what it measures.\n169|\n170|Confirmed equivalent (no observable outcome change, each checked rather than\n171|assumed): M09b/`ok`-in-required, R17 single-site, the closing-delimiter arm at\n172|`protocol.go:293` (unreachable after `More()` returns false),\n173|`splitFrame`'s oversize flag on the newline branch (measured: the frame reaches\n174|`DecodeResponse`'s identical bound and produces the identical arm —\n175|`provider_protocol_error` / \"frame exceeds 8 MiB\" / member `\"\"`), `isNull`\n176|widened to empty raw, and the base64 error disjunct (`raw` is nil on error, so\n177|the length arm refuses with the same detail).\n178|\n179|## Producer's reported facts — independently re-verified\n180|\n181|| Claim | Verified |\n182|| --- | ---: |\n183|| `go test ./... -count=1` exit 0, 15/15 | yes |\n184|| `-race` on provhost exit 0 | yes |\n185|| `go vet` and `GOOS=windows go vet` exit 0 | yes |\n186|| `go build` on host / linux / windows exit 0 | yes |\n187|| `gofmt -l` clean | yes |\n188|| `tracecheck` exit 0, 17/403 clauses, 74 acceptance cases | yes, exact |\n189|| provhost coverage 88.1% | yes, exact |\n190|| rev2 is test-only, production untouched | yes — `git diff 2c7022b 81f7c56` touches only LOGBOOK, README, and the three `_test.go` files |\n191|| the sweep no longer restores with `git checkout` | yes — `TASK-260830-qcosxq_round2-verify-sweep.sh` restores by `cp` from a backup, asserts the mutant was present, and byte-verifies the restore |\n192|| 18 killed / 2 declared equivalent in the producer sweep | consistent with mine; both equivalence declarations hold |\n193|\n194|## Standing bounds — unchanged, and one correction stands from round 1\n195|\n196|- **`internal/provhost` does not import `internal/provider`.** Unchanged: the\n197|  only reference is the comment at `runner.go:44`. My round-1 brief's\n198|  expectation that this leaf would be the first production caller was wrong;\n199|  leaf 1's bound is untouched.\n200|- **`internal/provhost` has no importer anywhere in the tree**, and there is no\n201|  `cmd/`. Two orphaned packages, no entry point. Every `Host.Call` assertion is\n202|  made from a test.\n203|- **Windows behaviour is compile-verified, not executed** — now actually\n204|  enforced in CI by the `GOOS=windows go vet` step this CR adds.\n205|- **The `Inspect`→`ReadFile` TOCTOU window is unprobed.** Unchanged.\n206|- `doc.go`'s non-retryability claim is now asserted on every refusal row through\n207|  both helpers — that round-1 bound is closed.\n208|\n209|## What this method cannot see\n210|\n211|Rows are derived by traversing production source, so a §7.2 duty with no code\n212|leaves no row and is invisible here; `tracecheck` reports 17/403 clauses\n213|discharged, and this review measures the quality of what was built, not the\n214|size of what was not. Concurrency beyond `-race` on the existing tests, and real\n215|Windows execution, are also outside it.\n216|\n217|## Verdict\n218|\n219|**changes requested → `to-dev`.**\n220|\n221|Blocking: **F30** (a fifth `parseMajor` branch deletable with an exit-code flip\n222|— the round-1 F21 defect on the arm round 1 did not name) and **F31** (a\n223|one-byte narrowing of `stdoutCap` that makes the host accept a frame followed\n224|by junk, measured through the production entry point).\n225|\n226|Structural (G-A): the enumeration is still manual. Two rounds have now each\n227|missed a row by hand. Rather than adding a sixth named `parseMajor` fixture,\n228|mechanise the enumeration the way the call-site audit already is: derive the\n229|`frameFault` literals and `integrity(...)` details from the AST and require\n230|each to have been *asserted*, not merely constructed — G1 and G3 are the two\n231|mutants that must go red. `parseMajor`'s branches need their own enumeration\n232|(they are classification branches, not arms), or a restructure that makes them\n233|enumerable.\n234|\n235|Also in the same round, all small: **X19**, **AS6** (fold `status_state` into\n236|`requireLocalRefusal`), and the `runner_test.go:484-487` comment correction.\n237|**S11/S12/S13/S4b** are acceptable as stated bounds if written down as such.\n238|\n239|Re-run bar for round 3: F30 and F31 killed; G1 and G3 red; no resurrection\n240|among the rows this verdict records as killed; and the report given as a\n241|killed/measured ratio over a production-derived traversal with equivalences\n242|argued, not asserted.\n243|\n244|## Harness\n245|\n246|`.temp/TASK-260830-qcosxq/mut2/` — `drive.py` (apply exact-once / full-package\n247|run / restore / re-derive the tree OID), `mut.py` (fails closed unless the\n248|pattern matches exactly once), `restore.sh` (`cp` from a pristine backup, never\n249|`git checkout`, and refuses to report OK unless the tree hashes back to\n250|`81f7c566`), `batch{1..4}.json` (the row set), `logs/` (one per mutant). Every\n251|run is full-package: `inventory_test.go`'s audit only fires when `-run` is\n252|empty, and it alone killed S3, S6, G2, and G4.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
